SUMMARY:[History Department] The Gatekeepers: Hamburg\, Germany and the Ma
 king of the Modern World
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261030T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261030T140000
DTSTAMP:20261029T184913Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-54136388471434-54136388473483@local-events
DESCRIPTION:The Gatekeepers is a history of the relationship between liber
 alism and globalization told through Hamburg\, the city known as Germany
 ’s “gateway to the world.” It tells the story of this city’s upper
  stratum\, a wide-ranging but remarkably durable network of people and ins
 titutions centered on Hamburg but spiraling out into the world. For one ce
 ntury\, this network committed itself to pulling Germany into the world ec
 onomy and to pulling the promises of an interconnected world into Germany
 —yet als\n\nAges: All ages\nSource: https://history.stanford.edu/events/
 gatekeepers-hamburg-germany-and-making-modern-world\nLast synced: 2026-10-
 29 11:49 PDT
LOCATION:Building 200\, History Corner
URL:https://history.stanford.edu/events/gatekeepers-hamburg-germany-and-ma
 king-modern-world
END:VEVENT
